WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.472
Structure-aware chunking.

2
00:00:01.752 --> 00:00:12.184
Many business documents already tell you where their pieces are: headings in Markdown and wikis, sections and clauses in contracts, slides in decks, rows in tables.

3
00:00:12.464 --> 00:00:19.056
Structure-aware chunking uses that structure as the primary boundary and the size limit as a secondary one.

4
00:00:19.336 --> 00:00:28.253
Split at headings first; if a section fits under the limit, it is one chunk; if not, split it recursively inside the section, never across a heading.

5
00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:32.480
Two extra benefits make this the default for structured corpora.

6
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:48.952
Each chunk inherits a heading path (“Employee Handbook > Time off > Sick days”), which you store as metadata, show in citations, and can prepend to the text before embedding so that a chunk saying “three consecutive days” carries the context that it is about sick leave.

7
00:00:49.232 --> 00:00:54.459
And section boundaries are where topics change, so chunks are more likely to be about one thing.

8
00:00:54.739 --> 00:01:08.861
LangChain ships a Markdown header splitter that does the first step and records the headers as metadata, and layout-aware parsers like Docling include chunkers that work on their structured document output, so you rarely start from nothing.

9
00:01:09.141 --> 00:01:14.965
You will write one yourself below anyway, because knowing exactly what the chunker does is how you debug it.

10
00:01:15.245 --> 00:01:31.971
Watch for three edge cases: a heading with no body (skip it; its title lives on in its children’s paths), very short sections (consider merging them with a neighbor so you don’t get a chunk that says only “See the travel policy”), and lines that look like headings but aren’t,

11
00:01:32.251 --> 00:01:36.219
such as a comment line starting with “#” inside a fenced code block.
